## The loader, as it stands now

`forth/boot.fth` is the source; `scripts/embed_forth.mjs` bakes it into the
image between `0x3C20` and `0x3FCC` (941 cells, the BDOS slice-file code
starts at `0x3FCD`). The baked block is at **921 cells**, so there is room
for a little more, but not much.

What changed:

- **`screen` walks the whole chain itself.** It reads one slice at a time
  into `slice-buf` and interprets it line by line with `evaluate`. The source
  text never enters the dictionary, so the chain can be as long as the flash
  holds (the old cap was eleven slices / 22,440 characters).
- **One colon word for the whole chain.** The kernel keeps the compile-time
  placeholders of `if`, `then`, `begin` and `do` on the return stack, so a
  loader word that returned between two lines would pop the *source's*
  placeholder instead of its own return address. Walking the chain in one
  word means there is no return in between. For the same reason the scan
  keeps its place in variables and the slice number never sits on the data
  stack while the source runs.
- **The line scan uses the kernel's `search`.** It finds the LF natively, so
  a line costs one native call instead of one interpreted step per character.
  The first version of this cut scanned cell by cell and booted in three
  times the steps; `search` brought that back down. `search` answers with the
  address of the match and the number of cells from the match to the end of
  the range, so the loader keeps the rest of the slice in a variable (`el`)
  and hands the next line a range that is one cell shorter. The range is the
  rest of the slice, so a slice that has no LF in it is not a source slice
  and the load stops there instead of running off into the dictionary.
- **The slicer cuts on line boundaries** (`sourceCuts` in
  `scripts/make_slices.mjs`), because a line that straddled a cut would be
  interpreted in two pieces. It also refuses a line over 128 characters,
  which `evaluate` could not read anyway.
- `text-buf` is gone; `p-screen` (packed type-8 slices) got its own
  `p-buf` and keeps using the kernel's `included`. The packed buffer is
  taken from the dictionary the **first time a packed slice is loaded**, not
  at boot: a board that never loads one does not pay for it, and a board
  that does pays for it once. That is worth 2,025 cells at boot (2,040 for
  the buffer, less the one cell the flag takes and the fourteen the extra
  code costs).
- **One kernel fix came with it.** An error inside an evaluation used to
  leave the evaluation stack alone: the kernel reported the error, read the
  next line, and at the end of *that* line resumed the thread of the text
  that had failed - the loader's own frame - so the rest of the source ran
  after the error that was supposed to stop it. The reader now clears the
  evaluation depth (`read_line` in `asm/forth_nano.asm`), so an error ends
  the line and every evaluation that was running with it. That is the
  standard's rule for an error, and it is what makes a failed load stop
  where it failed, the way the old `included` loader stopped.

  The fix sits in `read_line` and not in the error path itself because the
  error path lives in the `0x0100` code block, which has no words to spare:
  it ends at `0x0474`, and the `.org 0x0475` behind it would silently
  overwrite the tail - which is exactly what the first attempt did, and why
  the D16D image stopped booting with `PC=0`. `read_line` is in the `0x0500`
  block, and there the fix fits.

  It needed four words of room, and the `0x0500` block had none: at the
  loader-cut batch it ended at `0x18C2` in the segmented build, and the
  `.org 0x18C3` behind it - the BDOS word trampolines - was assembled
  last, so it overwrote whatever was there. The first attempt with the fix
  grew the block into that gap and destroyed the tail of `es!`: the
  editor's cursor store walked into the BDOS and the machine spun in a
  directory read, which is what the 41 failures in the segmented group
  were. `psw` is a leaf primitive that nothing calls from the interpreter,
  so it moved to the ten free words between the `0x0475` block and the
  `0x0500` one (`.org 0x04F6`). The `0x0500` block then ended at `0x18C1`,
  with two words to spare, and the same move kept the D16D build (which
  had one word) inside its gap. The phase 0 audit later moved the BDOS
  trampolines out of that gap altogether (they sit at `0x1FC2` now), so
  the fit is no longer tight.

## What was measured

D16D image, FPU profile, the real slice image in the flash. The phase 5
memory rework moved the dictionary base from `0x5800` to `0x3fc4` and the
stack top from `0xfff0` to `0xef80`, so the figures below are measured on
the current layout: the old loader (the `forth/boot.fth` of the strings
batch) and the new one, baked into the same kernel one after the other.

| | old loader | new loader |
|---|---|---|
| boot to prompt | 5,481,895 steps | 7,759,262 steps |
| dictionary after boot (`here`) | `0xbf9f` | `0x67ac` |
| dictionary limit | `0xef80` | `0xef80` |
| free dictionary cells after boot | 12,257 | 34,772 |
| source text in the dictionary | 22,528 cells (`text-buf`) | none |

So the loader costs about 42% more boot steps and gives back 22,515
dictionary cells: the 22,528-cell `text-buf` is gone, and the new loader's
own words and variables cost 13 cells more than the old loader's. The
packed loader's 2,040-cell buffer is not in this number: it is taken from
the dictionary only when a packed slice is loaded, and a boot loads none.

The old loader cannot load the compiler at all now: its `text-buf` holds
22,528 characters and the source is 27,482. With the compiler loaded from
the flash, the new loader leaves 24,016 cells free.

With the tables batch on top of the new loader:

| | after the loader | after the tables |
|---|---|---|
| free dictionary cells after `s" lila" need` | 25,491 | 24,016 |
| cells the compiler itself takes | 9,281 | 10,756 |
| data stack depth after the load | 0 | 0 |
| source | 22,226 chars | 27,482 chars |
| source slices | 11 (48–58) | 14 (48–61) |
| longest source line | 127 | 127 |

The first column is the loader-cut batch: the same boot with the smaller
compiler. Its free-cell figure is the boot figure above minus that
compiler, and its image size was measured before the phase 5 rework - the
image is 62 slices / 248 KB now.

The boot is unchanged by the tables - they cost dictionary cells, not boot
time.

In the D16SEG map the dictionary and the data stack share their cells: the
gap between the dictionary base `0x3fc4` and the stack top `0xef80` is
44,988 cells, and the free cells measured above are what is left of that
gap after the boot.

Tests: the whole suite is green on this tree (1035 tests after the phase 5
rework). The segmented group has a test of its own for the failed load: a
source slice with a bad word in the middle must run the line before it,
report the error, leave the line after it unrun, and leave the console
usable.

## Known limits of the current loader

- **A source line has to stay under 128 characters.** `evaluate` copies at
  most 128 characters into its buffer and throws `-22` above that. The
  longest line in the native profile is 127 characters, and the slicer now
  refuses to build a source with a longer line, so the failure comes with
  the build and not on the board.
- **The boot script has 20 cells of room** (921 of 941). Every line of
  `forth/boot.fth` costs `ceil(len / 2) + 1` cells, and an inline `\`
  comment costs as much as the code it sits on.
- **The chain is capped at 512 slices** (`en @ 511 >`), which is the whole
  flash; the cap exists to turn a corrupt link chain into a quiet stop
  instead of a hang.
- **The boot script's `create` does not reserve the data cell** (unlike
  `variable`): `create ea 0 ,` reserves it, `create ea` does not, and the
  next definition would land on top of it.
- `screen` still ends its line, so a module load has to be its own boot
  script line.
- **A failed load leaves its frame on the return stack.** The error path ends
  the line and abandons the evaluation, but it does not empty the return
  stack, so the loader's own frame (one cell) stays there - the old
  `included` loader left its own frame too. A later `r>` at the prompt would
  pick it up; the stack has thousands of cells, so this only matters after a
  load that failed.

## The tables batch

The host compiler (`lila/compiler.fs`, `lila/value.fs`, `lila/hash.fs`,
`lila/tablelib.fs`) has the tables; the native profile now has them too.

What it does:

- **tag 4** is the table. The value carries the address of the table's block
  of slots, and `value2` is zero.
- `{}` builds an empty table; `t[k]` reads (nil when the key is missing);
  `t[k] = v` inserts or updates; assigning nil deletes the key.
- `#t` is the *border* (the largest `n` with `1..n` all present), not the
  count, and it follows the host. `#s` is the length of a string.
- Keys are full tagged values - a number, a string, a boolean, a table or a
  float - and a nil key is rejected. Two keys match when their tags and their
  payloads are equal, which gives tables identity semantics, as on the host.
- Nested assignment (`t[k][j] = v`) is not supported on the native side
  either, as on the host; nested *reads* work.
- A table prints as `<table>`, where the host prints an address.

How it is built:

- **The pool is fixed.** `l-tslot` is 448 cells: eight tables of eight slots
  of seven cells each (`[k1][k2][ktag][v1][v2][vtag][occupied]`). The host
  uses a 4-cell header, open addressing with linear probing, growth at 75%
  and a rebuild on delete; the native version drops the header and the hash
  and scans the eight slots in order, which is enough for the fixed size.
- **Four new opcodes**: `TNEW` (26), `TGET` (27), `TSET` (28) and `LEN` (29).
  None of them has an operand.
- **The parser grew two rules.** `l-primary0` handles the constructor and
  `l-primary` loops over the indexes, so `t[a][b]` chains. The key is parsed
  as a whole expression, with `l-min` and `l-bop` saved and put back around
  it, because a key that changed the precedence would change how the *outer*
  expression is parsed. `#` is a new unary operator with the precedence of the
  unary minus, so it needed a new helper, `l-operand`.
- **The assignment has two forms.** A plain `name = expression` is the old
  path; `name[key] = expression` emits a `LOAD`, the key, and a `TSET`.

The new error codes are -27 (expected `}`), -28 (expected `]`), -29 (the
value is not a table), -30 (the key is nil), -31 (the table is full), -32
(too many tables) and -33 (`#` needs a string or a table).

Verification: 19 table programs agree with `lua5.4` 5.4.8 (the only
difference is `print(t)`, which is `<table>` here), the data stack is at
depth 0 after each of them, and all seven error codes fire. The regression
tests in `tests/native-lila.test.js` and `tests/native-lila-slice.test.js`
cover the same ground.

## What the tables batch left open

- **The pool is small and does not grow.** Eight tables of eight entries. A
  ninth entry in a table is `-31` and a ninth table in a session is `-32`.
  Nothing is reclaimed: the pool is reset when a new session starts, and
  until then a table that has had eight entries stays full.
- **There is no table constructor with contents.** `{1, 2}` is `-27`, as it
  is on the host.
- **Nested assignment is still rejected.** `t[k][j] = v` is `-21`.
- **There is no iteration over a table** - no `pairs`, no `ipairs`, no `next`,
  and no `table` library. The host has all of them.
- **A table is not equal to another table** by value; there is no `==` for
  tables, and comparisons are numeric only, as before.

The budgets are still inside: 24,016 free cells after the load, the source
is 27,482 characters in 14 slices, and the longest line is 127 characters.

## The next batch

The open question is what the profile grows next. The candidates are the
remaining language gaps - functions and `local`, the logical operators, the
bitwise operators, `for` loops - and the library gaps, which are larger
because the profile has no library at all. The table batch cost 1,475
dictionary cells, so functions with their frames would cost more again, and
the dictionary headroom is 24,016 cells.

Every batch keeps `lila/README.md`, `LUAPARITY.md`, `TYPEPLAN.md`,
`lila/LILANATIVE.md` and `lila/native/README.md` in step with the code, and
stays inside the flash and dictionary budgets above.

